<section class="reference-section" id="nachiketa-yama"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Nachiketa and Yama · The Secret of Death</h2><a href="/nachiketa-yama.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Katha Upanishad · Gita Press, Gorakhpur, code 578.</strong> Sanskrit text with Shankara-bhashya and Hindi explanation, from the owner-supplied 182-page scan. This is an original condensed English reader, not a translation. The scan is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>1.1.1–6</strong><small>Vajasravasa’s sacrifice, the worn-out cows, Nachiketa’s repeated question and the words sending him to Death. Supplied PDF pp. 18–24; printed pp. 6–12.</small></li><li><strong>1.1.7–11</strong><small>Three nights at Yama’s house and the first boon of reconciliation. PDF pp. 24–27; printed pp. 12–15.</small></li><li><strong>1.1.12–19</strong><small>The second boon and the fire that bears Nachiketa’s name. PDF pp. 29–34; printed pp. 17–22.</small></li><li><strong>1.1.20–29</strong><small>The question about death, Yama’s hesitation, the offered pleasures and Nachiketa’s refusal. PDF pp. 34–44; printed pp. 22–32.</small></li><li><strong>1.2.1–11</strong><small>Shreyas and preyas, ignorance, the qualified teacher and the prepared hearer. PDF pp. 51–59; printed pp. 39–47.</small></li><li><strong>1.2.15–23</strong><small>Om as support; the unborn and enduring Self; the limits of mere speech, intellect and hearing. PDF pp. 62–80; printed pp. 50–68.</small></li><li><strong>1.3.3–14</strong><small>The chariot image, the inward hierarchy and the subtle path. Printed pp. 73–83 in the supplied scan.</small></li><li><strong>2.3.9–18</strong><small>Inward perception, steadiness called yoga, the loosening of desires and the dialogue’s conclusion. PDF pp. 160–178; printed pp. 148–166.</small></li></ol><p>The reader uses “Self” for Atman and distinguishes it from the ordinary personality. It follows the supplied Shankara-bhashya edition while acknowledging that Vedanta traditions interpret the Upanishad differently. Yama is presented as teacher and Dharmaraja, not as a horror figure. The three images are newly AI-generated interpretations, each used once; people, architecture, objects and landscape are imagined. The reflection question is editorial.</p></div></section>
